1. The Spectral View (The Macro Window)

    What it does: Measures market structure across a slice of time (e.g., Fourier/wavelet transforms, Hurst exponents, principal component variance, or volatility clusters).

    Why you're attracted to it: Regimes don't exist in a single tick. A "trending," "mean-reverting," or "high-volatility" market is a wave property that requires a time window to detect.

    The trap: It trails reality. By the time a spectral window cleanly confirms a regime, the market might already be changing.

2. The Pointwise View (The Micro Trigger)

    What it does: Fires a discrete buy/sell order based on the exact current price, a immediate order book tick, or a single indicator crossing a threshold right now.

    Why you're attracted to it: You need a precise, zero-lag signal to actually enter the market and execute a trade.

    The trap: It is noisy. A single price tick knows nothing about the overall structure—it can easily trick your bot into buying a fake breakout during a dead regime.






